<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · Anatolia and the Levant</p><h1 class="st-title">Tarcondimotid Cilicia</h1><p class="st-meta">64 BCE – 17 CE (approx.) · capital Hierapolis-Castabala</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The Tarcondimotid kingdom, a Roman client state in the Amanus mountains of eastern Cilicia, loyal to Mark Antony.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tarcondimotus-i">Tarcondimotus</a>, a local ruler in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nur-mountains">Amanus</a> mountains of eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cilicia">Cilicia</a>, supported <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pompey">Pompey</a> when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> reorganised the region in 64 BCE and was recognised as dynast. He later took the title king, ruling from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hierapolis-castabala">Hierapolis-Castabala</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The kingdom controlled the passes between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cilicia">Cilicia</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/syrian-arab-republic">Syria</a> and the plain around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hierapolis-castabala">Castabala</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tarcondimotus-i">Tarcondimotus I</a> called himself Philantonios, friend of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mark-antony">Antony</a>, and supplied ships and troops to his patrons. Coins with his portrait and Greek titles survive.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tarcondimotus-i">Tarcondimotus I</a> died fighting for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mark-antony">Antony</a> in 31 BCE. Augustus later restored his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tarcondimotus-ii-philopator">Tarcondimotus II Philopator</a>, whose death in 17 CE led <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> to absorb the kingdom into its provinces.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="kingdom, 64 BCE – 17 CE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M22 72 V40 L36 54 L50 28 L64 54 L78 40 V72 Z"/><path d="M22 80 H78"/><circle cx="50" cy="24" r="3"/><circle cx="22" cy="36" r="2.5"/><circle cx="78" cy="36" r="2.5"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">kingdom</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Anatolia and the Levant</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:58.43%;width:1.61%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>64 BCE – 17 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/1st-century-bce">1st century BCE</a> – <a href="/centuries/1st-century">1st century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Hierapolis-Castabala</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Tarcondimotus I, Tarcondimotus II Philopator</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Greek and Anatolian religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Greek</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/turkey">Turkey</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Kingdom of Amanus, Tarcondimotids</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=-24&amp;focus=tarcondimotid-cilicia" aria-label="Tarcondimotid Cilicia on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5546 791 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Tarcondimotid Cilicia lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5304 942h0M5304 942h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5866 1047h0"/><path class="map-dot-ap" d="M5866 1047h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>Location approximate: shown in present-day Turkey. <a href="/atlas?year=-24&amp;focus=tarcondimotid-cilicia">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

